"[2610.37710] zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samarth Narang】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:SumCheck is a central prover primitive in zero-knowledge proofs, exposing table-parallel work that maps naturally to GPUs but becomes increasingly arithmetic-intensive as finite fields widen. We study how this shift affects the cost of programming SumCheck in Triton rather than CUDA.\nL17: Our benchmark, zkDuel, provides matched implementations of 25 zkPHIRE-derived constraints alongside diagnostic polynomials, covering 32-, 64-, 128-, and 256-bit prime fields, with independent CPU-reference validation and cross-backend checksum gating. Of 800 configurations attempted on an NVIDIA H100, 758 complete with matching checksums.\nL18: CUDA's median advantage over Triton falls from 3.5x at 32 bits to 1.4x at 256 bits, and Triton is faster on 19 large-table configurations; the same convergence appears on an A100, where the gap falls from 4.4x to 1.6x. Profiling shows that the small-field gap largely reflects host launch overhead and redundant modular additions in Triton's original block reduction, whose relative costs diminish as multi-limb Montgomery work grows.\nL19: Replacing that reduction with 【10†this http URL†tl.reduce】 brings Triton's median kernel time within 8% of CUDA on both GPUs, while specialized point interpolation improves 256-bit evaluation kernels by 1.55-1.75x. At cryptographic widths, these results show that a Python-embedded DSL can approach CUDA performance, although the largest fully unrolled constraints still expose compiler-scalability limits.\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted to HASP'26 at MICRO 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:2610.37710】 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or 【12†arXiv:2610.37710v1】 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.37710†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【14†https://doi.org/10.1145/3857307.3857312†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Samarth Narang [【15†view email】]\nL29: [v1] Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:28:29 UTC (51 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL38:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL39:   * 【16†TeX Source】\nL40: \nL41: 【17†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL42: \nL43: ### Additional Features\nL44: \nL45:   * 【18†Audio Summary】\nL46: ### Current browse context:\nL47: \nL48: cs.AR\nL49: \nL50: 【19†< prev】   |   【20†next >】\nL51: \nL52: 【21†new】 | 【22†recent】 | 【23†2026-10】\nL53: \nL54: Change to browse by:\nL55: \nL56: 【24†cs】\nL57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * 【25†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL63: \nL64: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL65: \nL66: ## BibTeX for