12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/391\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Zero-Knowledge IOPPs for Constrained Interleaved Codes】\nL32: \nL33: Alessandro Chiesa, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL34: \nL35: Giacomo Fenzi, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL36: \nL37: Guy Weissenberg, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Succinct arguments based on interactive oracle proofs (IOPs) have achieved remarkable efficiency improvements and are now widely adopted in applications. State-of-the-art IOPs involve protocols for testing proximity to constrained interleaved linear codes, and enjoy essentially optimal parameters. However, recent IOP constructions provide no privacy guarantees, which remain a must for many applications.\nL40: We present an IOP of proximity for testing constrained interleaved linear codes that achieves (honest-verifier) zero-knowledge, while incurring a negligible overhead compared to the (non-zero-knowledge) state of the art. In line with recent constructions, our construction satisfies round-by-round knowledge soundness with a straightline extractor and negligible error.\nL41: We propose a definition of (honest-verifier) zero-knowledge for interactive oracle reductions (IORs) that we prove is compatible with composition, and then obtain our result by constructing and modularly composing several lightweight zero-knowledge IORs.\nL42: Our key technical contributions are a zero-knowledge sumcheck IOR and a zero-knowledge code-switching IOR that fit the strict efficiency requirements of our setting; these contributions and other technical complications entailed overcoming several challenges with new notions and protocols. Finally, along the way, we highlight the efficiency benefits of high-distance codes obtained from dispersers, which may be of independent interest.\nL43: ##### Metadata\nL44: \nL45: Available format(s)\nL46:     【17†PDF】\nL47: Category\nL48:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL49: Publication info\nL50:     Preprint.\nL51: Keywords\nL52:     【19†interactive oracle reductions】【20†zero-knowledge】【21†linear codes】【22†dispersers】\nL53: Contact author(s)\nL54:     alessandro chiesa @ epfl ch\nL55: giacomo fenzi @ epfl ch\nL56: guy weissenberg @ epfl ch\nL57: History\nL58:     2026-02-26: approved\nL59:     2026-02-25: received\nL60:     【23†See all versions】\nL61: Short URL\nL62:     【24†https://ia.cr/2026/391†ia.cr】\nL63: License\nL64: \nL65: 【25†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL66: BibTeX 【26†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL67: \nL68:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/391,\nL69:           author = {Alessandro Chiesa and Giacomo Fenzi and Guy Weissenberg},\nL70:           title = {Zero-Knowledge {IOPPs} for Constrained Interleaved Codes},\nL71:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/391},\nL72:           year = {2026},\nL73:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/391}\nL74:     }\nL75: \nL76: \nL77: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\np3-sumcheck 0.8.0 - Docs.rs (https://docs.rs/crate/p3-sumcheck/latest)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/crate/p3-sumcheck/latest\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 72\nL0: # p3-sumcheck 0.8.0\nL1: \nL2: Sumcheck protocol engine (product polynomials, SVO, HVZK masks)\nL3: \nL4:   * 【0†Crate】\nL5:   * 【1†Source】\nL6:   * 【